course: arts
lesson: 19
title: Street art and graffiti: legal walls vs vandalism
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Street art is art in public places, and the big question is always the same: did the owner agree? A huge mural on a block of flats, paid for by the council, is legal and often loved; a name sprayed on a bus shelter without asking is vandalism.
explain: Picture Callum's mate spraying his tag (a quick signature) on the bus shelter and saying "it's just art, innit": the next day a council worker scrubs it off, paid for with everyone's taxes.
explain: Many cities have legal walls where anyone can practise graffiti, and good murals are planned on paper first, with a sketch, a grid to scale it up, and the owner's agreement.
explain: Some street art becomes famous, like Banksy's stencils, and some towns protect loved graffiti behind glass while neighbours argue "It's art!" "It's a mess!"; but train tracks are never a place for art, because fast, quiet trains and electrified rails kill.
explain: Street art has its own slang: "bombing" is graffiti slang for spraying lots of tags fast, a "piece" is a big detailed graffiti painting, a "writer" is a graffiti artist, and "ayo" means hey, what.
fact: A mural is a big wall painting done with permission, often paid for by councils, building owners or festivals.
fact: Writing or spraying on property without permission is vandalism; someone has to pay to clean it, often the town using taxes, and getting caught can mean fines or worse.
fact: Some cities have legal walls, also called free walls, where graffiti is allowed, and the paintings change quickly.
fact: Banksy is a British street artist whose name and face are kept secret; his stencils often joke about war, money and power.
fact: In 2018 one of Banksy's paintings partly shredded itself right after it sold at auction.
fact: Since 2014, and especially after 2022, many murals in Ukrainian cities show hope, heroes and national symbols; Banksy painted several works in and around Kyiv in 2022.
fact: In graffiti slang, bombing means spraying lots of tags quickly, usually illegally; it has nothing to do with real bombs.
fact: Railway lines are deadly: modern trains are fast and quiet, some rails carry deadly electricity, people have died going onto tracks to paint, and trespassing on railways is illegal.
fact: Good murals start with a plan: a sketch, a grid of squares to copy a small picture large, and agreement with the owner.
fact: 3D pavement art uses perspective tricks so a chalk drawing looks deep from one spot; chalk washes away in the rain.
fact: Some graffiti becomes so loved that towns protect it behind glass, while others think all graffiti should go; councils must decide, and opinions are split.
exercise: Dylan sees a huge whale painted on a block of flats. Who allowed it? | The council paid for it, so it is a legal mural | Nobody, because all street painting is illegal everywhere | The whale painted itself overnight | The tenants did it secretly at night without asking anyone
exercise: Callum's mate sprays his name on the bus shelter. What is it? | Vandalism, damage that someone must pay to clean | Legal art, because he called it art and signed his name | A mural, because it is on a wall | A gift to the town council, which should thank him for it
exercise: Who often pays to clean graffiti off public places? | The town, using taxes | The bus company's passengers only | Nobody, it washes off by itself in the rain | The graffiti writer, always
exercise: Isabella's legal wall changes every week. What is a legal wall? | A wall where graffiti is allowed | A tall wall built around a law court | A wall nobody may touch | A wall that only the police may paint
exercise: Sam asks who Banksy is. What is true? | A British street artist whose identity is kept secret | A famous Italian painter who lived in the 1500s | A cartoon character on TV | A council team that cleans graffiti off the walls
exercise: What happened to a Banksy painting in 2018? | It partly shredded itself right after selling at auction | It was painted over by the council by mistake one night | It was stolen from the Louvre | It was found hidden deep in an old Welsh mine
exercise: Mykola's grandad says the wall used to be grey and sad. What does the mural do now? | Helps the community remember and feel proud | Breaks the law, because it is a painting in Kyiv | Hides the wall from tourists | Shows only adverts for the shops and cafés on the street
exercise: Who painted several works in and around Kyiv in 2022? | Banksy | Leonardo | Andy Warhol | Michelangelo
exercise: Abbie thinks "bombing" means real bombs. What does it mean? | Graffiti slang for spraying lots of tags fast | Throwing real bombs at walls to knock them down | Painting pictures of bombs | Dropping tins of paint from planes onto the town
exercise: Ji-ho thinks painting by train tracks looks cool. What is true? | Fast, quiet trains and electrified rails kill people | You can always hear a train coming in plenty of time | Tracks are safe if you are quick | Trains always stop for artists working by the line
exercise: Chen's art club plans for weeks. How do good murals start? | A sketch, a grid to scale it up, and the owner's agreement | Spraying freely and just seeing what happens | Waiting for night so nobody sees them painting | Copying an advert from a shop without asking anyone at all
exercise: What is a grid used for in mural painting? | To copy a small picture large | To stop people touching the wet wall | To hold up the ladder | To keep the paint dry in the rain
exercise: Jasmine's friend nearly steps in a chalk "hole". What is it? | 3D pavement art that looks deep from one spot | A real hole covered over with chalk dust | A drain painted by the council | A deep crack left over from an old earthquake
exercise: Ellie's neighbours argue "It's art!" "It's a mess!" What is true? | Opinions are split, and councils must decide | Everyone agrees that all graffiti is art | Everyone agrees all graffiti must go | Graffiti is never discussed by any council at all
exercise: What does a graffiti "writer" mean? | A graffiti artist | A newspaper reporter | A person who writes books | A council cleaner
test: Your mate goes, "if you call it art, it's legal, innit." What is the truth? | Without the owner's permission it is damage | He's right, the word "art" makes it legal | Only spray paint is illegal, so felt pens are fine | It is legal if nobody sees you doing it
test: What would happen if Callum's mate got caught tagging? | He could face fines or worse, even as a kid | Nothing, because kids are never punished | The council would pay him | He would get a medal from the mayor for brightening the town
test: Abbie panics when her brother talks of "bombing". What should she know? | It's slang for illegal tagging, not real bombs | He is going away to fight in a war | It means painting a mural legally | It means throwing water balloons at his friends
test: A film hero sprays a masterpiece on train tracks and jumps clear at the last second. What is the truth? | Modern trains are quiet and fast, and people have died this way | You can always jump clear in time if you are fit | Trains slow down for artists | The rails are safe to touch, even the electric ones
test: Jasmine's friend says "Ayo, I nearly stepped in it!" Why did she think it was a hole? | Perspective tricks make chalk look deep from one spot | There was a real hole hidden under the chalk | The chalk was wet cement | It was a puddle
test: You love graffiti and want to practise. What is the wise move? | Find a legal wall or a class | Spray your school at night | Paint near the railway | Tag bus shelters for practice
test: What would happen if Chen's club painted the school wall with no plan and no permission? | It could be damage and look messy | It would be legal because it is their school | It would be better than a planned one | Nothing at all
test: Sam's dad says "it might be a Banksy". Why can't they just ask Banksy? | His name and face are kept secret | He lives on the Moon | He only speaks Latin to people | He died back in the 1500s
test: Ellie's two neighbours shout at each other about the graffiti. What is a good way to think it through? | Listen to both sides before deciding | Pick whoever shouts the loudest | Paint over it yourself | Ignore both of them for ever
test: Your cousin says street art is always against the community. What do Ukrainian murals show? | Murals are often made with and for the community | Street art always attacks the town and its people | Murals are always illegal | Only tourists like murals, never the people living there
test: Is all street painting illegal? | No, murals with permission are legal | Yes, every wall painting is a crime | Yes, unless it is in chalk only | No, because walls belong to nobody
exam: Identify the principal criterion distinguishing a lawful mural from an act of vandalism. | The permission of the owner of the property | The artistic merit of the image | The colour of the paint employed | The time of day at which it is painted
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the term "bombing" in the vocabulary of graffiti. | The rapid and usually unlawful application of numerous tags | The use of explosive devices against buildings and their walls | The lawful painting of commissioned murals | The depiction of military aircraft
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hazard presented by railway lines? | Trains are fast and quiet, and certain rails carry lethal electricity | A train may invariably be heard in sufficient time for any person to withdraw | Rails are harmless to the touch | Trains halt automatically for pedestrians
exam: Ascertain the customary procedure in the preparation of a large mural. | A sketch, a grid for enlargement and the agreement of the owner | Spontaneous painting without design or consent of any party | Painting by night to avoid observation | The copying of an advertisement without permission of its owner
